% Values: lists, key order, scalar context, indexing, deep comparison.

### name: val.list.build
--- source
(3, 2, 1)
--- expect
tree -{"1"=t"3", "2"=t"2", "3"=t"1"}
===
### name: val.list.append-idiom
--- source
A = ("a", "b"); A = (A, "c"); A
--- expect
tree -{"1"=t"a", "2"=t"b", "3"=t"c"}
===
### name: val.list.flattens-and-renumbers
--- note
A list operand contributes its children's values, never its keys (§5.9).
--- source
A = ("x", "y"); B = (A, A); B
--- expect
tree -{"1"=t"x", "2"=t"y", "3"=t"x", "4"=t"y"}
===
### name: val.list.grouping-passes-one-argument
--- note
F((1,2)) is one list argument; F(1,2) is two.
--- source
COUNT((1, 2))
--- expect
num 2
===
### name: val.scalar-context.first-child
--- source
A = (7, 8); A + 0
--- expect
num 7
===
### name: val.scalar-context.assignment-then-index
--- source
A[1] = 3; A == 3
--- expect
bool TRUE
===
### name: val.scalar-context.recurses
--- source
A[1][1] = 5; A + 0
--- expect
num 5
===
### name: val.scalar-context.empty-has-none
--- source
FILTER((1, 2), _ > 5) & "x"
--- expect
error E_NO_SCALAR
===
### name: val.index.verbatim-key
--- note
The index is used as a key exactly as written: A[1] and A[1.0] differ.
--- source
A[1] = "a"; HAS(A, "1.0")
--- expect
bool FALSE
===
### name: val.index.missing-key
--- source
A = (1, 2); A[3]
--- expect
error E_NO_KEY
===
### name: val.index.on-scalar
--- source
A = 3; A[1]
--- expect
error E_NO_KEY
===
### name: val.undefined-variable
--- source
FOO + 1
--- expect
error E_UNDEF_VAR at 1:1
===
### name: val.reserved-word-as-variable
--- source
IN
--- expect
error E_RESERVED
===
### name: val.assign.autovivifies
--- source
A["x"]["y"] = 1; A
--- expect
tree -{"x"=-{"y"=t"1"}}
===
### name: val.assign.keeps-key-position
--- note
Re-assigning an existing key keeps its original position; order is normative.
--- source
A[1] = "a"; A[2] = "b"; A[1] = "c"; A
--- expect
tree -{"1"=t"c", "2"=t"b"}
===
### name: val.count.scalar-has-no-children
--- source
COUNT("abc")
--- expect
num 0
===
### name: val.indexes
--- source
A["x"] = 1; A["y"] = 2; INDEXES(A)
--- expect
tree -{"1"=t"x", "2"=t"y"}
===
### name: val.has
--- source
A["x"] = 1; HAS(A, "x") XOR HAS(A, "z")
--- expect
bool TRUE
===
### name: val.eql.structural
--- source
(1, 2) EQL (1, 2)
--- expect
bool TRUE
===
### name: val.eql.is-not-numeric
--- note
EQL compares structurally, so scale is visible where == would ignore it.
--- source
"5.00" EQL "5"
--- expect
bool FALSE
===
### name: val.eql.key-order-matters
--- source
A["x"] = 1; A["y"] = 2; B["y"] = 2; B["x"] = 1; A EQL B
--- expect
bool FALSE
===
### name: val.eql.kind-matters
--- source
"A" EQL FROM_HEX("41")
--- expect
bool FALSE
===
### name: val.in.list
--- source
2 IN (1, 2, 3)
--- expect
bool TRUE
===
### name: val.in.absent
--- source
"PL" IN ("DE", "CZ")
--- expect
bool FALSE
===
### name: val.in.scalar-compares-directly
--- source
"x" IN "x"
--- expect
bool TRUE
===
%
% --- identity and aliasing (spec/SPEC.md §3.4)
%
% Evaluating an expression yields a value, not a snapshot of one, so a mutation
% made by a later sub-expression is visible through a reference obtained
% earlier. Assignment is the only thing that copies.
%
% Four of these were found by the differential fuzzer, on seeds outside the
% default run. Every host but one already agreed on all eight; the C++ host
% deep-copied on the read path where the others alias, which made three of them
% E_NO_KEY, one E_NO_SCALAR, and one — the last — a confidently wrong value.
% Two are controls that already passed everywhere: they are here to stop a fix
% for the others from over-reaching.
### name: alias.index-expr-creates-the-key-it-reads
--- note
The index expression creates the key that the pending read then finds. This is
§3.4's first paragraph, and the smallest case that shows it.
--- source
A = 1; A[A["k"] = "k"]
--- expect
text "k"
===
### name: alias.index-base-from-a-call
--- note
The base is not a variable or an index chain, so it cannot be recovered by
re-walking a path — IF returns the value of its argument, and that value is the
same one the index expression mutates. An implementation that fixes the case
above by resolving a path will still fail this one.
--- source
A = 1; IF(TRUE, A, A)[A["k"] = "k"]
--- expect
text "k"
===
### name: alias.index-base-grouped
--- note
Parenthesising the base groups it; it does not copy it.
--- source
A = 1; (A)[A["k"] = "k"]
--- expect
text "k"
===
### name: alias.index-base-is-a-real-copy
--- note
CONTROL — this already passes everywhere and must keep passing. B was assigned
from A, and assignment is the one operation that copies (§5.7), so mutating A
must not be visible through B. If a fix for the cases above makes this succeed,
the fix has confused aliasing with re-reading.
--- source
A = 1; B = (A); B[A["k"] = "k"]
--- expect
error E_NO_KEY at 1:18
===
### name: alias.aggregate-binder-is-the-element
--- note
The binder names the element, not a copy of it, so a body that mutates the list
changes what a later read of the binder sees. The first element gains a child
before COUNT(X) runs on it; the second does not, because the mutation targets
element 1 both times.
--- source
A = (1,2); MAP(A, X, (A[1]["c"] = 9) & COUNT(X))
--- expect
tree -{"1"=t"91", "2"=t"90"}
===
### name: alias.compound-target-read-across-the-rhs
--- note
The compound forms hold their target across the evaluation of the right-hand
side, so a right-hand side that adds a child to the target changes what scalar
context then finds. A[x] starts as a childless NONE — scalar context on it would
be E_NO_SCALAR — and the right-hand side gives it the child that makes the
concatenation succeed.
--- source
A = 1; A["x"] = FILTER(1, FALSE); A["x"] &= (A["x"]["c"] = "z")
--- expect
text "zz"
===
### name: alias.rebinding-is-not-visible-to-a-pending-read
--- note
CONTROL — this already passes everywhere and must keep passing. It is the other
half of §3.4: the index expression rebound A to a three-element list, but the
pending read still holds the two-element value A had when it was evaluated, and
that has no key "3". Mutating a value is visible; replacing a variable is not.
--- source
A = (1,2); A[(A = (A, 9)); "3"]
--- expect
error E_NO_KEY at 1:13
===
### name: alias.compound-inside-an-index
--- note
The one that returns a wrong answer rather than an error when a host gets this
wrong, which is why it is worth having even though it overlaps the cases above.
--- source
A = 1; A["k"] = 1; A[(A["k"] += 1); "k"]
--- expect
num 2
===
